{"A":"Refine the strongest measured direction: the A candidate averaged 0.00600 s versus the 0.01188 s baseline. Investigate incremental improvements to contiguous adjacency storage, one-time neighbor-ID resolution, and iterative path-sum reuse. Measure changes independently and preserve first-ID-match behavior, neighbor order and tie handling, positive-value selection, query spelling, and output formatting.","B":"Continue the independent ID-lookup and data-layout line. The packed-ID SIMD candidate averaged 0.00858 s, faster than baseline but slower than A; compare lookup alternatives against A’s sorted index and test whether they improve end-to-end time, not just lookup. Preserve earliest-match semantics for duplicate IDs and handle arbitrary valid IDs without relying on test-specific ranges.","C":"Explore parallelism at a different granularity from query-level OpenMP: the measured query-parallel candidate averaged 0.01197 s, slightly slower than baseline. Test whether independent per-node successor-selection/preprocessing work can be parallelized profitably on the four-core ARM64 target, with deterministic tie behavior and safe handling of shared state. Retain a serial path where measurements show parallel overhead outweighs gains.","G":"Treat A’s 0.00600 s candidate as the performance lead, not a proven winner: the supplied measurements explicitly report correctness for baseline, not for each candidate. Verify correctness before integration. Integrate one independently useful change at a time, then measure the complete integrated program; do not assume gains add, and do not carry over query-level parallelism without evidence. Preserve behavior for every valid input and the exact output contract; retain only integrations that are both correct and measurably faster.","analysis":"The source repeatedly scans node IDs and neighbor strings, then recursively recomputes sums along a selected single-neighbor path. A’s contiguous adjacency, indexed ID resolution, and iterative reuse align with those costs and have the best observed mean. B’s packed-ID SIMD lookup is a promising but slower alternative to A. C’s query-level parallelism showed no gain over baseline, motivating tests of other work granularity rather than simply adding threads. Any integration still requires correctness checks and fresh end-to-end timing."}